{"i": "For {proj}, '{goal}' adds delegation: users grant scoped access to delegates. Decompose delegation as an authority-with-expiry problem.",
 "a": "Decompose authority explicitly: a grant record with scope, ceiling, and expiry, constraint evaluation intersecting grantor and grant, audit events on grant use distinct from self-actions, revocation reachable instantly and visibly from every surface, and verification simulating scope-escalation attempts. Delegation widens identity; it is never session copying. Every grant in the plan carries expiry, audit, and a death story."},
{"i": "For {proj}, '{goal}' add field-level permissions to API responses. Decompose against the compute-and-cache risks of shape-varying responses.",
 "a": "Decompose with policy-visible fields: field visibility computed at serialization through the central policy layer, cached payloads scoped per visibility class or only over visibility-stable data, negative probes proving blocked fields never serialize, and performance witnesses per visibility class. Shape-varying responses are cache poisoning and compute storms waiting to harmonize. Decomposition puts policy in one choke point, with caches allergic to concealed variance."},
{"i": "For {proj}, '{goal}' requires admin impersonation of customers for support tooling. Decompose with audit and consent-safe constraints.",
 "a": "Decompose as bounded-elevation: explicit impersonation sessions minted per ticket, strong visual banner visible to the operator and audit-event distinct from real-user sessions, write-action ceilings inside impersonation, automatic expiry, and verification proving no path elevates silently. Support elevation is a control, not a hack. Impersonation without audit trails is an insider feature, not a support feature."},
{"i": "For {proj}, '{goal}' introduces hierarchical permissions: org, project, resource. Decompose inheritance without surprise overreach.",
 "a": "Decompose the inheritance semantics first: which permissions inherit downward, which are explicitly revocable at lower levels, conflict resolution between levels, effective-permission computation available as debug surfaces, and adversarial verification at boundary objects. Hierarchies amplify mistakes downward. Unexpected inheritance found in production was defined in ambiguity, proven here."},
